course: dictionary
lesson: 11645
title: Rude words: arse
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words are rude. Grown-ups use them in some places and some books, but you should not say them at school. This lesson helps you understand one of them, so you know what it means when you meet it.
explain: «Arse» is a rude word in British, Irish, Australian and New Zealand speech. Its first meaning is the bottom, the part of your body you sit on. At school, say bottom or bum instead.
explain: The word also means a stupid or annoying person, as in «He is a pain in the arse.» Phrases like «can't be arsed» mean too lazy to do something. Context helps you spot these meanings, but you should not use the rude word in your own talk or writing.
fact: «Arse» is a rude swear word in British, Irish, Australian and New Zealand English.
fact: «Arse» is ruder than «bum», which is milder, and about as rude as «ass» in American English.
fact: «Arse» can mean the bottom, or a stupid or annoying person.
fact: «Can't be arsed» means too lazy or not interested enough to do something.
fact: «Arse about» or «arse around» means to waste time by being silly.
exercise: At school, which is the polite word for the part of your body you sit on? | Knee | Elbow | *Bottom | Ankle
exercise: «He said he can't be arsed to tidy his room.» What does can't be arsed mean? | Very keen to tidy his room | *Too lazy or not interested enough to do it | Lost his room key | Angry with his room
exercise: «Stop arsing about and get on with your work.» What does arsing about mean? | *Wasting time by being silly | Asking the teacher for help | Working very hard | Sitting down quietly
test: «He is such an arse, he never listens.» What does arse mean here? | The bottom, the part you sit on | *A stupid or annoying person | A kind helper | A very fast runner
test: Why is it good to avoid rude words like arse at school? | They help you spell better | *They can upset people and they are not polite | They are only used in Scotland | They make sentences longer
exam: Which is the best meaning of «can't be arsed» in «I can't be arsed to walk to the shop»? | Too cold to go outside | Too tired to read the shop sign | Too happy to walk fast | *Too lazy to walk to the shop
